From 2b8da93e061614d42e0fc0de4a345afa78e2ae5d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shiro <x@x>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 01:54:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/10] wasm speed and memory: one CPU under the GIL, one host
 block per file mapping, native 64-bit atomics

- GetCpuCount() reports 1 while the interpreter lock is on: more CPUs only
  made runtimes like Go start threads that contend for the lock.
- emscripten: a file-backed guest mapping is one host mmap() block whose
  pages are reference-counted, instead of one host mmap() per 4 KiB page
  (each of which allocated and zeroed a 64 KiB-aligned block in a proxied
  call). gh 2.62 (59 MB): peak RSS 999 MB -> 272 MB.
- CAN_ATOMIC64: wasm32 has lock-free 64-bit atomics, but Blink treated it
  as a 32-bit host and wrapped every guest memory access and page-table
  load in a mutex. Interpreter loops ~30% faster.
